EDA Insights:
=>Histograms show the distribution of numerical features and help identify whether the data is normally distributed or skewed.
=>Boxplots help detect outliers in the dataset. Some features contain extreme values, which may influence SVM performance.
=>The correlation heatmap shows relationships between variables. Features with higher correlation may have stronger influence on predicting the drug response.
=>Pairplot visualization helps understand class separation between different drug response categories.
=>Countplot indicates whether the target variable is balanced or imbalanced across classes.

Model Performance Insights:
=>The SVM model using the RBF kernel achieved good classification accuracy, showing that nonlinear decision boundaries are effective for this dataset.
=>The classification report provides precision, recall, and F1-score for each class, indicating how well the model predicts different drug response categories.
=>The confusion matrix shows the number of correct and incorrect predictions and helps identify misclassified classes.

Hyperparameter Tuning Insights:
=>GridSearchCV was used to find the best combination of parameters such as C, kernel, and gamma.
=>Tuning improves model performance by selecting optimal parameters for classification.

Kernel Comparison Analysis:
=>Linear kernel works well when data is linearly separable.
=>Polynomial kernel captures more complex relationships but may increase computation time.
=>RBF kernel generally performs best because it handles nonlinear patterns effectively.
=>Comparing kernel accuracies helps identify the most suitable kernel for the dataset.

Conclusion:
=>The assignment successfully demonstrates EDA, visualization, SVM classification, model evaluation, and hyperparameter tuning.
=>Among different kernels, the best-performing kernel provides improved prediction accuracy for drug response classification.
=>Proper interpretation of visualizations and model comparison improves understanding of the dataset and machine learning performance.